> Is it possible to access a save made on one computer from another?

Short answer: Maybe.

Long answer: It depends on which platform you're running on, which 
version you're using, how your binary was compiled (compiler used, 
compiler options, compile-time switches), and what patches you're 
using.

* Each platform uses a different save format. You can't use Win32 saves 
on a Linux build, for example.
* All other things being equal, version 3.4.3 can use save and bones 
files from any 3.4.x version, but not any earlier ones (so a 3.4.1 save 
should work, but a 3.3.1 save definitely won't).
* If your binary uses a different compiler, compiler switches, or 
compile-time options from the one that created the save file, it likely 
won't work. You can still try, of course, but don't bet on it working.
* Lastly, some patches change the save format (can't think of any 
offhand, though).

If you're using an official binary, you don't have to worry about the 
last two bullets. If you're compiling a binary yourself, though, you 
need to keep those in mind.

